Firecrawl just published[a walkthrough](https://www.firecrawl.dev/blog/mastra-browser-logged-in-pages) for` @mastra/browser-firecrawl` , the browser provider their team built for Mastra. It gives your agent a hosted browser for pages behind a login, with no Chromium running on your machine. Their demo logs into Sauce Labs' test store and checks out a backpack.


Their post covers setup. We'll walk through what happens inside Mastra when the agent logs in. One detail matters once you move from the demo to a real login: where the password ends up.


## Same tools, different browser


` FirecrawlBrowser` extends our[AgentBrowser](https://mastra.ai/reference/browser/agent-browser) , so the model drives the page the same way with either one. Both give the agent the same 16 browser tools by default. The only difference is where Chromium runs.` AgentBrowser` launches it on your machine with Playwright.` FirecrawlBrowser` gets a session from Firecrawl and connects to it over CDP.


Each browser session belongs to a thread. Inside` @mastra/agent-browser` , every page tool pulls` threadId` from the agent's execution context before running:


browser/agent-browser/src/tools/type.ts


```text
import   {   createTool   }   from   "  @mastra/core/tools  "  ;
import   type   {   AgentBrowser   }   from   "  ../agent-browser  "  ;
import   {   typeInputSchema   }   from   "  ../schemas  "  ;
import   {   BROWSER_TOOLS   }   from   "  ./constants  "  ;


export   function   createTypeTool  (browser  :   AgentBrowser  )   {
return   createTool  ({
id: BROWSER_TOOLS.TYPE,
description:
"  Type text into an input element. Use clear: true to replace existing content.  "  ,
inputSchema: typeInputSchema,
execute  :   async   (input, { agent })   =>   {
const   threadId   =   agent?.threadId;
browser.  setCurrentThread  (threadId);
await   browser.  ensureReady  ();
return   browser.  type  (input, threadId);
},
});
}
```


` agent.threadId` is the same thread ID you pass to` memory` when calling the agent. The first browser tool call on a thread opens a Firecrawl session; subsequent calls on that thread reuse it. When a user follows up with "now check out" on turn two,` Memory` loads the prior conversation and` FirecrawlBrowser` reconnects to the session that still holds the logged-in cookies and cart. Two users on different threads get isolated histories and separate browser sessions.


The map from thread to session lives in your server process, though. If the server restarts or a request lands on another instance, the thread gets a fresh browser with no cookies. By default, Firecrawl also ends a session after five minutes idle or ten minutes total.


## The password ends up in your traces


When the agent logs in, the model calls` browser_type` with something like this:


```text
{   "  ref  "  :   "  @e2  "  ,   "  text  "  :   "  secret_sauce  "   }
```


That's a tool call. Mastra treats it like any other. Memory stores it with the thread's messages, and tracing records it as a span.[ToolCallFilter](https://mastra.ai/reference/processors/tool-call-filter) can keep old tool calls out of the prompt, but the stored copy stays.


Our[sensitive data filter](https://mastra.ai/reference/observability/tracing/processors/sensitive-data-filter) runs on traces by default, but it matches on field names like` password` and` token` . The browser tool's field is called` text` . So the password lands in your traces as plain text.


That's harmless with Sauce Labs' public test login. With a real account like your ops team's vendor portal, you want the password out of the agent entirely. Firecrawl's named profiles handle this. A person or a setup script opens a session on a profile with` saveChanges: true` and logs in once. Closing that session saves the login. Your agent opens the same profile with` saveChanges: false` and starts out signed in. The model never sees the password, so it never reaches your memory or your traces. And since the login lives in the profile, it survives server restarts and expired sessions too.


On the agent, set` saveChanges: false` explicitly. Firecrawl[defaults it to true](https://docs.firecrawl.dev/api-reference/endpoint/browser-create) , and only one session can write to a profile at a time. With the default on, a second thread that opens the profile at the same time gets a 409.


## Get started


```text
npm   install   @mastra/browser-firecrawl
```


Set` FIRECRAWL_API_KEY` , pass a` FirecrawlBrowser` to your agent's` browser` field, and follow[Firecrawl's walkthrough](https://www.firecrawl.dev/blog/mastra-browser-logged-in-pages) for the full demo. The[FirecrawlBrowser reference](https://mastra.ai/reference/browser/firecrawl-browser) has every option.


Thanks to the Firecrawl team for building this.
